## Landed semantics

- F-249 **Under walk, a program that imports only the prelude reaches the string objects' own `||` only through `String`'s abstract `opr ||(self, b:String)`.** The string objects' own `||` are those that the objects `FlatString` and `EmptyString` declare, and that the trait `Concatenable` declares for the objects `CatString` and `SubString`. Code inside `FortressLibrary` reaches `FlatString`'s own `||` directly, because `FortressLibrary` imports `FlatString`'s api, which declares it. This bears on one proposed change: to move `String`'s `||` members to top-level declarations over `(Any, String)` and `(String, Any)`, as the library declares `juxtaposition`. Their meet, the top-level declaration over `(String, String)` that the Meet Rule requires, must then delegate to the string objects' own `||`. `FortressLibrary`, where the meet is declared, sees those only where an api that it imports states them: `FlatString`'s api does, and the probe added five lines to `String.fsi` for `Concatenable`'s and `EmptyString`'s. Otherwise every concatenation of two strings in such a program runs the meet's own body. With the body that the probe tried, `Concatenable`'s, `"s" || "t"` gives a `CatString` of depth 1, one level of concatenation, where the library today gives a `FlatString` of depth 0, with the same printed value. The route and these results were each observed once, in probes on a patched library; no gated test holds them. Detail: `perf-probes/symbolic-operators/REPORT.md` sections 2.2 and 2.4.
  by: the clarity pass; note: rewritten for a reader without the probe (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f14/proposals.md, "F-249"); the title scoped to a program that imports only the prelude, as section 2.4 states it, with FortressLibrary's own direct route; "juxtaposition's device" said in plain words and the pair that the meet covers named (section 2.2, ways JC and JCp); the kind of evidence said: one probe run on a patched library (way JCp); fix round: the string objects named as the source names them (FlatString and EmptyString, and the trait Concatenable for CatString and SubString; String.fsi:16, :22, :28); the body narrowed to the one that the probe tried (way JCp); the imported api tied to FortressLibrary, where the meet is declared, and the five String.fsi lines (sections 2.2, 2.4); depth defined (FlatString.fss:42)
- F-252 **Under walk, where the parameter-by-parameter check refuses two declarations with static parameters of their own, the load check reads the pair again on its declared domains, by the No Duplicates, Return Type and Meet Rules.** A declaration with static parameters of its own is a generic function, a generic object's constructor, or a functional method that declares static parameters in a trait or object that has none. Walk's load check first compares two overloads parameter by parameter, and a pair that it finds excluding loads. It reads a refused pair of two such declarations again, each over all its own instances, and loads the pair if it meets the three rules. A pair that the parameter check orders is read again only for the No Duplicates Rule (F-?1). F-?2 holds the Return Type Rule. The parameter check does not find two instances of one generic type at different ground static arguments excluding, such as `Tg[\3\]` and `Tg[\4\]`, so two plain declarations so typed are refused (row 416). For the Meet Rule, walk looks for a third declaration that covers the overlap only where, at each position, the two parameter types are equal, or one of them is a static parameter, or both are instances of one generic type whose static arguments match in the same way (row 539). A pair whose domains exclude when read whole also loads, where neither declaration has a `where` clause of its own (F-258; row 787). A pair with a varargs parameter is not read again and keeps the parameter check's refusal (F-257). At a call, walk compares a functional method of a generic trait or object on the instance that the call makes. It compares a generic dotted method in the same way, except where exactly one of the two declared domains lies inside the other; there it chooses the inner one (F-253). Gated by `tests/DispatchRenamedGenericArmWalk`, `DispatchSwappedGenericArmWalk` and `GenericPairNestedBoundsWalk` (load and dispatch), and the expected failure `XXXOverloadExistentialMeetWalk` (row 539). Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-generic-pairs/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 5.
  by: the clarity pass; note: reorganized (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f14/proposals.md, "F-252"); split into this entry, F-?1 (equal declared domains, No Duplicates) and F-?2 (the Return Type Rule at every instance), which change apart; "own generic" said in plain words from ownGeneric's comment (OverloadedFunction.java, rung-existential-reduction/REPORT.md section 1); the title scoped to the pairs that the parameter check refuses, from genericPairValid (OverloadedFunction.java:897); moved here: the code sites genericPairValid (cited as :766, which is validOnDeclaredDomains; genericPairValid is at :894 now), :533-540 and sameDeclaredDomains :806 at 6d187d069, returnRuleAtEveryInstance at 36afa7eb1; fix round: the where-clause condition restored to the whole-type reading (domainsExcludeTogether, :943; row 787); the ordered pair's No Duplicates reading named (:538-540); the row 416 sentence bounded to plain declarations, pending the walk run of F-155's pair; the Meet search said as matchApart does it (:1288-1313); the dotted method's exception given its outcome, the inner one (rung-walk-method-sargs/REPORT.md section 1, part B); "parameter lists of different lengths" dropped, since such a pair is never compared (:376-381)
- F-?1 **Under walk, two declarations whose declared domains are equal are refused by the No Duplicates Rule, where one has static parameters of its own and the other has its own or none, whether or not the parameter-by-parameter check orders them.** The parameter check reads a type parameter as strictly below its bound. So it orders `h[\T extends Number\](x: T, y: T)` before `h(x: Number, y: Number)`, though their declared domains are equal. Walk compares the declared domains, each declaration's over all its own instances, and refuses the pair: "fails because their parameter lists have the same types" (F-257). A type parameter bounded by `Any` counts: walk refuses `g[\T\](a: T)` beside `g(a: Any)`, and the compiled checker accepts that pair (row 759). Gated by `tests/GenericBesideGenericSameDomain`, `GenericBesidePlainSameDomain`, `NoDuplicatesRuleWalk` and `NoDuplicatesRuleSizeWalk` (refused). Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-generic-pairs/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 5; `compile-ladder/rung-existential-reduction/REPORT.md` section 5 (row 759 on both paths).
  from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-walk-generic-pairs/REPORT.md, "1. What changed and why"; by: the clarity pass; note: split from F-252 (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f14/proposals.md, "F-?1"); the example h from section 5's table; NoDuplicatesRuleWalk and NoDuplicatesRuleSizeWalk, which F-257 already names, added as gates of the refusal of a pair that the parameter check refuses (genericPairValid's NO_DUPLICATES)
- F-?2 **Under walk, where the parameter-by-parameter check refuses two declarations with static parameters of their own and one lies inside the other, the load check reads the Return Type Rule at one instance of the outer one, and refuses the pair unless that instance stands for every instance that applies.** The instance is the one that walk's inference finds for the outer one at the inner one's parameter types. A check at that instance alone would load pairs that the rule refuses, since an argument of the inner one can select a smaller instance of the outer one. The instance stands for every applicable instance in two cases. In the first, each static parameter that the outer one's return type names is fixed by a static argument of one of its parameter types. In the second, the outer one returns one of its own type parameters, and the inner one meets one of two conditions. Either it returns a type parameter of its own that is the whole type of at least one of its parameters and appears in no other parameter type than those at positions where the outer one's parameter type is that returned type parameter; or, at such a position, it has an object type below which its return type lies. Otherwise walk refuses the pair, "fails the Return Type Rule" (F-257). With `trait Shape`, `trait Round extends Shape` and `object Circle extends Round`, it refuses `f[\T extends Shape\](x: T, y: T): T` beside `f[\U extends Round\](x: U, y: Round): Round`, and `f[\T extends Shape\](x: T): Box[\T\]` beside `f[\U extends Round\](x: U): Box[\U\]`; the compiled checker refuses both too. Gated by `tests/GenericReturnRuleOuterParamWalk` and `GenericReturnRuleInvariantWalk` (refused) and `GenericReturnRuleHoldsWalk` (load and dispatch). Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-generic-pairs/SKEPTIC.md` section 2.
  from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-walk-generic-pairs/SKEPTIC.md, "2. The fix: the Return Type Rule at one instance (a defect of the change)"; by: the clarity pass; note: split from F-252 (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f14/proposals.md, "F-?2"); the reason for the every-instance condition from the skeptic's finding; the code site returnRuleAtEveryInstance at 36afa7eb1 left to that section; fix round: the parameter check's refusal restored to the title (genericPairValid is reached only at :530-531, :770); the instance named as the one that walk's inference finds (instanceHolding, :901-902); the second case said as returnRuleAtEveryInstance reads it (:1103-1121); Shape, Round and Circle named (tests/GenericReturnRuleOuterParamWalk.fss:6-8)
- F-253 **Under walk, a call of a generic method that writes no static argument infers the method's own static arguments from the arguments' run-time types.** This holds for a dotted call, as for a call of a generic function, whether the method is alone under its name or among its receiver's overloads. Among the receiver's overloads, where one of two is a generic dotted method and exactly one of their declared domains lies inside the other, walk chooses the inner one; otherwise it compares them on the instance that the call makes (F-252). A generic method that a trait declares reads its bounds, and the types to which its arguments convert, in the trait's method environment, where its instances bind and evaluate their types, as a method that an object declares does. So it takes the instance that the same method declared in an object takes: a `ZZ32` and a `ZZ64` give `Box[\ZZ64\]` with the `ZZ32` converted, and a bounded type parameter that nothing fixes takes its bound. A generic functional method that an object inherits from a trait is chosen beside the object's plain one where only it applies. Where the inference with conversion finds no instance, as for an argument outside a bound, walk falls back to unification. For a trait's method, the unification still reads the trait's member environment, which has no parent, as the team's code did. So it does not read the bound, and the call fails with 'Cannot unify Int ... with Shape ... abm=K=(Int,Any)'; the same method declared in an object fails with 'Meet(Int, Shape) not a singleton'. This inference lets the team's generic `distribute` over `K` run nested reductions under walk (F-248). Gated by `tests/MethodStaticArgInference.fss`, `MethodStaticArgOverloadedReceiver.fss`, `MethodStaticArgDeclaredDomain.fss`, `NestedReductionFusion.fss`, `TraitGenericMethodInference.fss` and `InheritedGenericFunctionalMethodBesidePlain.fss`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-method-sargs/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 2; `compile-ladder/rung-walk-trait-method-bounds/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 4, and its `SKEPTIC.md` section 3.
  by: the clarity pass; note: rewritten for a reader without the batch (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f14/proposals.md, "F-253"); the title widened from a dotted call to a call of a generic method, since the body also covers an inherited generic functional method (row 765, rung-walk-trait-method-bounds/REPORT.md section 1); "as an own generic is" dropped; "Rung L's generic distribute" named as F-248 names it; moved here: GenericMethod.getTypeEnv (GenericMethod.java:106), EvaluatorBase.java:491, :567, :646, :651 and :886, all at 5c5dee93d; fix round: the dotted method's choice said with its outcome (rung-walk-method-sargs/REPORT.md section 1, part B); the trait's method environment restored as the place where the bounds are read (rung-walk-trait-method-bounds/REPORT.md section 1); the member environment defined, and the unification, not the error, said to read no bound
- F-257 **Under walk, a pair of declarations that the load check refuses after it reads the pair on its declared domains is refused in the name of the rule that the pair breaks.** The load check reads a pair on its declared domains where its parameter-by-parameter check refuses the pair and both declarations have static parameters of their own, or one has them and the other is a plain declaration (F-252, F-27). It reads a pair that the check orders only for the No Duplicates Rule (F-?1). The Return Type Rule: "fails the Return Type Rule: the first [second] is more specific, and its return type is not found to be a subtype of the return type of every instance of the second [first] that applies to its arguments". The Meet Rule: "fails the Meet Rule: neither is found to be more specific than the other or to exclude it, and no declaration is found that is more specific than both and applies to every call to which both apply". The No Duplicates Rule: walk's "fails because their parameter lists have the same types". The messages say "not found" because walk's reading is narrower than the rules: rows 539, 550, 416 and 779 hold valid pairs that walk refuses. A pair that is not read on its declared domains keeps the parameter check's message, such as "with generic type, at least one pair of parameters must have excluding types". Such a pair has a varargs parameter, or parameter lists of different lengths, or raises an error while its instances are read. Or it is of neither kind above, as a functional method of a generic trait beside another, or it is the pair of instances that an explicit instantiation such as `f[\A, B\]` makes of two generic declarations of one name (`XXXGenericOverload2`). The message does not decide which pairs load. Gated by `tests/GenericReturnRuleInvariantWalk`, `GenericReturnRuleOuterParamWalk` and `GenericBesidePlainReturnRuleWalk` (Return Type), `GenericOverloadInstancesApartWalk`, `GenericBoundsNoMeetWalk` and `GenericBesidePlainNoMeetWalk` (Meet), and `NoDuplicatesRuleWalk` and `NoDuplicatesRuleSizeWalk` (No Duplicates). Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-trait-method-bounds/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 3.
  by: the clarity pass; note: reworded (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f14/proposals.md, "F-257"); "Which pairs load is unchanged", a comparison with the code before climb batch 18's rung W, restated as a present property; "own generic" said in plain words; the four rows given their common subject; moved here: ruleFailure, OverloadedFunction.java:817-839, validOnDeclaredDomains :766 and genericPairValid :891, all at 5c5dee93d, and the DomainReading type; fix round: the parameter check's refusal restored as the condition of the reading (:530-531), with the ordered pair's No Duplicates reading (:538); the explicit instantiation said with its example (XXXGenericOverload2.fss:21-24); a pair that raises an error while its instances are read added (REPORT section 3; :797-799, :915-917)

## The library's arrays and algebra

- F-154 **The library declares its matrix and vector products with shared `nat`s, so under walk a product of the wrong shapes stops the run at dispatch.** A shared `nat` is a size parameter that two parameter types name, as `m` in `DOT` and juxtaposition of `Matrix[\T,n,m\]` by `Matrix[\T,m,p\]`. The matrix-vector and vector-matrix products share theirs in the same way. Each product name is overloaded, so the stop reads "Failed to find any matching overload", with the argument types and their sizes. It is a `ProgramError` that no `catch` takes. A declaration that is not overloaded, such as the APL model's `pick`, fails instead with a unification error that names the size; no library product is such a declaration. Observed under walk for a mismatched `DOT` (row 254), and in a probe of the model's own sized declarations, where a `try` around a mismatched call caught nothing. Detail: `apl/mg/NOTES-swap.md`, "What the shared nats catch: the mismatch probe".
  by: the clarity pass; note: reworded (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f14/proposals.md, "F-154"); each message tied to the declarations it was observed at: the unification error at the model's pick, case 7 of the mismatch probe, which is not a library product; every library product name is overloaded (FortressLibrary.fsi:1777-1800)
- F-157 **An array is a trait with `get` and `put`, and a view is an object that implements it over other storage; this is the language's own idiom for a view of a runtime shape, as the library's own `TransposedMatrix` is.** Under walk, a subtype of `Matrix` that overrides `mul` gets its own product through the library's `Matrix` juxtaposition and `DOT`, which forward to `mul`. It needs no api line, since walk dispatches the method on the receiver's run-time type; the override was measured under walk only. In two measurements under walk at `b176a4b79`, views through the library cost more. On lifting a function over the rows of a 64 × 16 matrix, the row slice `m[i,:]` took five times as long as a six-line user view (row 298). The library's product with a diagonal that is a plain `Matrix` view, without the override, took 13 times as long as a row-scaling operator at n = 16, and 58 to 63 times at n = 64 (row 299, which says 54). Detail: `reviews/c4-flatarrays-review.md` section 1 (the idiom); `run-c4/probes/vocabulary/diag-override.md`, "Result" (the override) and "Timings against row 299" (the ratios).
  by: the clarity pass; note: reworded (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f14/proposals.md, "F-157"); both costs bounded to their measurements, tree and sizes (rows 298 and 299, at b176a4b79); "the library's operators" narrowed to the two that forward to mul (diag-override.md, the probe's opening paragraph); fix round: the idiom cited to reviews/c4-flatarrays-review.md section 1, with its condition, a view of a runtime shape, and the library's TransposedMatrix; the n = 64 ratio corrected to the measured 58 to 63 (diag-override.md, "Timings against row 299"; row 299 says 54); the override scoped to walk (diag-override.md: "Interpreter path only"); the row lift and the plain view said in words
